          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em relative adjoint functor
            (with respect to a functor
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            %em relative coadjoint functor
            is a generalisation of the notion of adjoint functor, in which the domain of the right adjoint is not required to be the same as the codomain of the left adjoint. In both cases, when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
          %p
            In generalization of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "monad#RelationBetweenAdjunctionsAndMonads"} relation between adjunctions and monads
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monads"} relative monads
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+comonads"} relative comonads
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Fix a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J\colon A \to E
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R \colon C \longrightarrow E
          %p
            to have a
            %em
              left
              = succeed "-relative" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                left
                = succeed "-adjoint" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi J
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L \colon A \longrightarrow C
          %p
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C\big(L(-),-\big)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  E\big(J(-),R(-)\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Such a situation is called a
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              \-relative adjunction
            and is denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi J
